  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 147 PM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThis afternoon through tonight...\n\nCurrent KIND radar imagery depicts increasing coverage of showers \nacross central IN after a brief lull in precipitation.Increasing \nlarge scale ascent from an approaching mid-upper level vort max \ncentered over IL combined with low-level theta-e advection ahead of \nan associated low pressure system will continue to promote \nadditional convective development. Expect widespread showers this \nafternoon along with scattered thunderstorms once sufficient \ndestabilization occurs. \n\nEfficient rainfall rates and repeated rounds of showers/storms from \nthe slow moving system could result in localized flash flooding \nthrough daybreak Sunday. An associated warm front lifting north is \nexpected to stall near or just north of the I-70 corridor into the \nevening hours which may also increase the threat for flooding as \nslow moving storms could train over the same areas. A FLOOD WATCH is \nnow in effect for much of the forecast area through 8 AM EDT Sunday \nwith rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches expected. Locally higher \namounts of 4+ inches are possible.\n\nModest to moderate destabilization later this afternoon combined \nwith sufficient effective wind shear supports the threat for \nisolated severe thunderstorms, mainly for E/SE portions of central \nIndiana. Forecast soundings depict gradually steepening lapse rates \nand mid-level dry air contributing to DCAPE values around 900 to \n1100 J/KG which may result in isolated damaging wind gusts. Low-\nlevel veering hodographs from the warm front lifting north could \nalso promote an isolated tornado threat.\n\nSome uncertainty still remains regarding the overall severe threat. \nMesoscale details such as how far north the warm front moves and the \ndegree of destabilization or low-level buoyancy later this afternoon \nwill ultimately determine the severe weather risk. The flooding \nthreat is the greatest concern, but isolated severe weather remains \npossible over mainly E/SE portions of central Indiana. If any severe \nweather occurs it would likely be from around 2 to 9PM EDT before \ninstability weakens.\n\nSunday through Monday...\n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system is expected to still be over \nthe area on Sunday as it slowly crawls southeastward. Broad cyclonic \nflow and lingering moisture will promote scattered or numerous \nshowers. A low flooding threat may linger into Sunday, but very \nlimited instability and weaker forcing should lead to lower rainfall \nrates. Look for coverage of showers to decrease late in the day into \nSunday night with the system gradually departing. \n\nSurface high pressure building in Monday will provide mostly quiet \nweather. However, lingering moisture and daytime heating may support \nisolated showers or a stray storm across far southeastern counties \nlate in the day.\n\nTuesday onward...\n\nLong range guidance generally shows the aforementioned system \nbecoming cutoff once again and retrograding towards the Ohio Valley \nTuesday into Wednesday. Deeper moisture advecting back into the \nsoutheast half of central Indiana along with weak dynamics from the \nsystem and diurnal heating keeps low chances for precipitation in \nthe forecast, mainly during the afternoon or evening. Multiple \nembedded shortwaves traversing the region Thursday through end of \nthe week will promote additional convective chances. Exact details \nremain uncertain at this time due to diverging model solutions.",
